aquinas-known-necessary.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered no, which keeps aquinas-known-necessary.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "the future contingent thing is known by God", "even what we ourselves know must necessarily be" and "the knowledge of God is much more certain than ours" are true and "necessarily, the future contingent thing is (comes to be)" is false. Is S2, "3: Further, everything known by God must necessarily be, because even what we ourselves know, must necessarily be; and, of course, the knowledge of God is much more certain than ours.", true then?
aquinas-known-necessary.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: aquinas-known-necessary.logic.r2 and aquinas-known-necessary.logic.r2.2 have "our_known_necessary ∧ god_more_certain → (known_by_god → □be)"; aquinas-known-necessary.logic.r2.3 has nothing from it; they differ when only "the future contingent thing is known by God", "even what we ourselves know must necessarily be" and "the knowledge of God is much more certain than ours" are true, which the second allows
